+    <section id="roasters-journal" class="journal-section">
+      <div class="journal-header">
+        <h2>Roaster's Journal</h2>
+        <button type="button" id="summarize-btn" class="btn summarize-btn">Summarize</button>
+      </div>
+
+      <article id="journal-article" class="journal-article">
+        <h3 class="journal-subtitle">From High-Altitude Farms to Your Morning Cup: How We Source Our Beans</h3>
+        <p>
+          Every bag of coffee at The Daily Grind begins its journey thousands of feet above sea level on family-owned smallholder farms across the coffee belt. Each harvest season, our green-coffee buyers travel directly to origin in Ethiopia's Yirgacheffe region, Colombia's Huila highlands, and Guatemala's volcanic Huehuetenango valleys. By building multi-year direct-trade relationships rather than buying anonymously through commodity exchanges, we pay farmers a guaranteed premium averaging 45% above Fair Trade baseline prices—ensuring living wages, community reinvestment, and long-term agricultural stability.
+        </p>
+        <p>
+          We partner exclusively with growers who practice shade-grown, regenerative agriculture. Beneath canopies of native banana, inga, and avocado trees, coffee cherries mature slowly in mineral-rich volcanic soil without synthetic pesticides. This slower ripening concentrates natural sugars and organic acids inside the seed, producing the vibrant floral aromatics, stone-fruit sweetness, and crisp citrus acidity that define specialty-grade Arabica.
+        </p>
+        <p>
+          During harvest, pickers selectively hand-pick only peak-red cherries at 21 to 24 degrees Brix sugar content. Depending on the regional microclimate and target flavor profile, lots undergo either crystal-clear mountain-spring washed fermentation or raised-bed natural sun drying, where cherries are raked hourly for up to three weeks to prevent uneven moisture levels.
+        </p>
+        <p>
+          Before a single burlap sack inside protective GrainPro liners is shipped to our roastery, every micro-lot undergoes rigorous physical grading and blind cupping protocols. Lots must score 86 points or higher on the Specialty Coffee Association scale and pass moisture and water-activity checks. Once the green beans arrive at our local roastery, we craft tailored small-batch roast curves to highlight each farm's distinct terroir and ship within 24 hours of roasting.
+        </p>
+      </article>
+
